# Every subject every contract in this repository declares or produces, with the population's rows
# for it counted by verdict. One row per (contract, subject).
#
# What a contract is, counted
#   A contract is a roster, a bare `VALUES` literal naming its subjects, and a population, rows that
#   each name a subject and, for some contracts, carry a verdict. The roster says which subjects
#   there should be. Everything this repository asks of a contract is a count per subject: whether
#   a declared subject has no rows, whether a row names a subject nobody declared, how many rows
#   carry a verdict, whether any came back silent. This relation gives those counts once, for every
#   contract, with the subjects that have no rows printed, because the roster drives.
#
# Each contract's outer join on its own, then their union
#   Each arm composes one count from folds/, the roster fully outer-joined to the population and
#   grouped per subject, and each keeps the counts its own verdict has. The union keeps the columns
#   every contract shares, and this file, holding every arm, names each one; no count knows it has
#   siblings.
#
# The two sides of the outer join
#   `declared` and `rows > 0` are the two sides: declared with no row is a roster naming something
#   no population produces, and a row that is not declared is a population growing past its roster.
#   Both false cannot occur, because every row here comes from one side or the other.
#
# `answered` for a population with no verdict
#   The rules, the arithmetic sites and the laws carry a verdict, and count `answered`, `silent` and
#   `vacuous` in their own files. The others carry none: a catalogue row naming a table is the whole
#   answer to whether the table exists, so the union gives them `rows` as `answered`, and zero for
#   `silent` and `vacuous`, because nothing in them can be withheld. Every reader treats a row of
#   those populations as its own answer, so saying so merges nothing a reader tells apart.
#
# Who reads it
#   reports/integrity.sqlc crosses it with the problems a contract can have and keeps the ones
#   found; rank/guard_reach.sqlc counts, per contract, the declared subjects some answered row
#   reaches. algebra/integrity.sqlc and algebra/subject_boxes.sqlc hold every count but the laws'
#   own from outside; the laws' count cannot be held from algebra/, whose relation it counts.
#
# What `classes` declares, and why its roster is not the type
#   The other contracts govern things somebody wrote here: a rule, a site, a law, a table, a
#   diagram law, a typed-blank column, a derivation column. `classes` governs the members of the
#   types in assets/ddl/schema.ddl, so its population, like the diagrams', is written by Postgres
#   rather than by these files. Its roster cannot be `enum_range`: nothing can land outside a type,
#   so the side *produced, but nothing declares it* could never have a row, and the count would
#   have one side instead of two. epistemics/class_domain.sqlc is written by hand for that reason,
#   and the disagreement it can then report is between two files that share no code,
#   assets/ddl/schema.ddl and itself.
-- every contract's count, each labelled with the contract it counts.
SELECT 'rules' AS contract, f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.answered, f.silent, f.vacuous
FROM ( :compose(folds/rule_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'arithmetic', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.answered, f.silent, f.vacuous
FROM ( :compose(folds/site_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'algebra', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.answered, f.silent, f.vacuous
FROM ( :compose(folds/law_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagrams', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.rows, 0::bigint, 0::bigint
FROM ( :compose(folds/object_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagram laws', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.rows, 0::bigint, 0::bigint
FROM ( :compose(folds/diagram_law_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'absences', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.rows, 0::bigint, 0::bigint
FROM ( :compose(folds/absence_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'derivations', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.rows, 0::bigint, 0::bigint
FROM ( :compose(folds/derivation_subjects.sqlc) ) f
UNION ALL
SELECT 'classes', f.subject, f.declared, f.rows, f.rows, 0::bigint, 0::bigint
FROM ( :compose(folds/class_subjects.sqlc) ) f
